    <p class="eyebrow mt-16" style="color:#0057B7">The scale of harm</p>
    <h2 class="h-sec mt-3 mb-5">Lives, homes, and a country's future</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">The UN Human Rights Monitoring Mission has verified at least 16,431 civilian deaths since 24 February 2022, and stresses that the true number is significantly higher, since it can only count what it can independently confirm. The World Health Organization has verified more than 3,000 attacks on healthcare in Ukraine — hospitals, maternity wards, ambulance teams and pharmacies among them. The Kyiv School of Economics Institute, working with Ukraine's Ministry for Development of Communities and Territories, estimates direct war damage to infrastructure at roughly $170 billion as of November 2024, with 236,000 residential buildings damaged or destroyed and Chernihiv named among the regions hit hardest. Nearly 5.9 million Ukrainians are refugees abroad and a further 3.7 million are displaced inside the country — together, close to a quarter of Ukraine's pre-war population forced from their homes.</p>
    <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2 text-sm">
      <a href="https://ukraine.ohchr.org/en/UN-Human-Rights-Monitors-Warn-of-Rising-Civilian-Casualties" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-bold underline underline-offset-2">OHCHR civilian casualties</a>
      <a href="https://www.who.int/europe/news/item/08-05-2026-3000-attacks-on-health-care-in-ukraine-verified-by-who-since-full-scale-invasion" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-bold underline underline-offset-2">WHO healthcare attacks</a>
      <a href="https://kse.ua/about-the-school/news/damages-to-ukraine-s-infrastructure-due-to-the-war-have-risen-to-170-billion-kse-institute-estimate-as-of-november-2024/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-bold underline underline-offset-2">KSE Institute damage assessment</a>
      <a href="https://www.unhcr.org/us/emergencies/ukraine-emergency" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-bold underline underline-offset-2">UNHCR Ukraine emergency</a>
    </div>

    <p class="eyebrow mt-16" style="color:#0057B7">Children taken, futures stolen</p>
    <h2 class="h-sec mt-3 mb-5">The fate of Ukraine's children</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">Ukraine's National Information Bureau has registered nearly 20,000 Ukrainian children forcibly deported or displaced to Russia or Russian-occupied territory, separated from their families and communities — of those, around 17,000 are individually identified, with their place of origin and current location known. In March 2023, the International Criminal Court issued arrest warrants for Vladimir Putin and Russia's children's rights commissioner, Maria Lvova-Belova, over the war crime of unlawfully deporting these children. As above, the Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe has gone further and recognised these deportations as genocide outright.</p>
    <div class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2 text-sm">
      <a href="https://www.icc-cpi.int/news/situation-ukraine-icc-judges-issue-arrest-warrants-against-vladimir-vladimirovich-putin-and" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-bold underline underline-offset-2">ICC arrest warrant</a>
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Child_abductions_in_the_Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-bold underline underline-offset-2">Documentation of child deportations</a>
    </div>

    <p class="eyebrow mt-16" style="color:#0057B7">Accountability, underway but not enough</p>
    <h2 class="h-sec mt-3 mb-5">Justice on the horizon, not yet protection</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">Ukraine has twice accepted the International Criminal Court's jurisdiction to investigate atrocity crimes on its territory, covering alleged war crimes, crimes against humanity and genocide since November 2013. Ukraine's own Prosecutor General's Office has registered more than 190,000 suspected war crimes cases. These processes matter — but for people living under bombardment, occupation or displacement right now, accountability is still a promise on the horizon, not yet protection in their daily lives.</p>
    <a href="https://gp.gov.ua/en/posts/ponad-190-tisyac-vojennix-zlociniv-ofis-genprokurora-predstaviv-komisiyi-oon-masstabi-zlociniv-rf-proti-ukrayini" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-ink mt-6">Ukraine's Prosecutor General's Office {% icon "arrow-ur", "w-4 h-4" %}</a>

    <p class="eyebrow mt-16" style="color:#0057B7">Not abstract to us</p>
    <h2 class="h-sec mt-3 mb-5">This is our team's daily reality</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">Our founding team and colleagues live and work in Chernihiv right now, running youth programmes in a city under regular air raid alerts. This war is not history: people across Ukraine live with the threat of drone and missile strikes as part of daily life, checking for alerts before school runs, work, or sleep. You can see this in real time — <a href="https://alerts.in.ua/en" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="font-bold underline underline-offset-2">alerts.in.ua</a> tracks live air raid alerts across every region of Ukraine. We're still working on collecting and sharing our Ukrainian team's own words here directly — write to us if you'd like to hear from them.</p>
    <a href="https://alerts.in.ua/en" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-ink mt-6">See live air raid alerts {% icon "arrow-ur", "w-4 h-4" %}</a>

    <p class="eyebrow mt-16" style="color:#0057B7">Four countries, one network rooted in Ukraine</p>
    <h2 class="h-sec mt-3 mb-5">Why we speak plainly</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">Elysium+ is a European youth network rooted in Chernihiv, working across Ukraine, Poland, Italy and Germany. We build safe spaces where young people — many of them living through war, exile or return — can organise, heal, and claim their right to shape their communities' future. We cannot talk about "mobility" and "participation" without naming the violence that has tried to strip Ukrainians of both. Euphemisms do not protect anyone from missiles, deportations or torture; they only make it easier for outsiders to look away.</p>

